{
  "programId": "com.digitalarsenal.flows.geonames-ingest",
  "name": "GeoNames Gazetteer Ingest Flow",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "engineLinkage": "bridge",
  "description": "Scheduled ingest of a gazetteer into $GNP. A 24 h host-cron TICK enters at geonames-ingest:mark_query, which builds the flatsql-query that reads this dataset's durable resume mark; hostcap/flatsql-query returns it to geonames-ingest:ingest_plan, which decides between three outcomes and only three. FIRST TICK (no mark) = the SEED: one ranged hostcap/http-request for cities15000.zip plus three more for the division and country files, because $GNP carries a RESOLVED admin and country NAME beside every code and those names live in separate files. EVERY LATER TICK = the DELTA for that tick's own UTC day: modifications-YYYY-MM-DD.txt and deletes-YYYY-MM-DD.txt, verified live 2026-08-15 to be full 19-column geoname rows and three-column tombstone rows respectively - so the delta upserts through the SAME decoder as the seed and there is no second record shape to keep in step. A TICK FOR A DAY ALREADY ON THE MARK emits nothing at all: the mark IS the same-data ledger, carrying the upstream ETag that also rides out as If-None-Match, so an unchanged file costs one conditional request and comes back 304, which geonames-source:inflate ends the run on without an error. The seed archive is inflated IN-GUEST (vendored miniz; there is no host unzip capability and none may be added) and the chunk is bounded by the Go server host's 4 MiB response-body cap, which ingest_meta re-checks against Content-Range's denominator rather than trusting. geonames-ingest:ingest_meta authors the storage attribution before hostcap/storage-ingest persists it through the policy-mediated storage.ingest_with_source cap op, with reconcile=append because a daily modifications file is an upsert of a few thousand rows and not the gazetteer's set. geonames-ingest:publish_request then advances the mark FROM THE STORAGE RESULT - never at dispatch, so a crash between fetch and store re-fetches one day instead of skipping it invisibly - and emits the epoch announcement only when a publish URL is configured; DATASET_EPOCH and DATASET_CID already ride in $GNP.SOURCE, so no new record type is minted. The tombstone list leaves the flow on egress: no host capability removes a stored row, and inventing one would be a new host capability this lane may not add. The host owns no schedule, no dataset list, no licence and no record semantics: it contributes timers, HTTP egress, guarded persistence and the FlatSQL row that holds the mark. No new host connector is required.",
